    "text": "1/250 s handles walking people; 1/500–1/1000 s handles running and most sport; 1/2000 s and beyond freezes birds, water splashes and ball strikes. Handheld, keep the shutter no slower than 1/focal-length seconds unless stabilisation helps."
  },
  "after": {
    "label": "deliberately blurred",
    "text": "Panning a cyclist at 1/30 s keeps the subject sharp while the world streaks — speed made visible. Long exposures turn waterfalls to silk and traffic into light rivers (tripod mandatory; in daylight a neutral-density filter buys the missing stops). Stars run 10–25 seconds before Earth's rotation smears them."
  },
  "why": "Blur is a vocabulary, not a mistake — the skill is knowing the freezing speeds well enough that what you keep is what you meant to keep."
};

const expMeterSpot = {
  "prompt": "One line of this student's metering notes is wrong — click it:",
  "lines": [
    "The built-in meter is a reflected-light meter: it reads the light bouncing off the scene.",
    "Its fixed assumption: the scene, on average, reflects middle grey.",
    "Snow photographs come back grey because the meter underexposes the white toward grey — add about +2 stops of compensation.",
    "Evaluative/matrix metering meters a tiny circle — the tool for deliberately placing one tone.",
    "The histogram graphs brightness from left (black) to right (white)."
  ],
  "errorLine": 3,
  "correction": "That describes spot metering; evaluative/matrix reads the whole frame against a database of scenes, and centre-weighted privileges the middle.",
