---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/photography/foundational/the-exposure-triangle">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the first lesson in Photography. You need nothing from earlier lessons — only a
    camera (a phone will do) and the everyday experience that some photos come out too dark,
    too bright, too blurry or too busy. Everything else is built here, step by step.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>explain what the three exposure dials — aperture, shutter speed and ISO — each control, and how the classic bucket analogy maps onto them;</li>
    <li>read f-numbers correctly, including why the scale runs backwards, and count light in stops;</li>
    <li>trade exposure between the dials: convert one correct exposure into another using the stop as currency;</li>
    <li>predict each dial's side effect — depth of field, motion blur, noise — and choose dials on purpose for portraits, landscapes, sport and night work;</li>
    <li>explain what the light meter assumes, why snow photographs turn grey, and how exposure compensation and the histogram put you back in charge.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit: whenever you look at a photograph you admire, ask <em>which side effect the
    photographer chose</em> — the dissolved background, the frozen wingbeat, the silk-smooth
    waterfall — and then work out what that choice cost on the other dials. Every exposure is
    a trade; learning photography is learning what to trade.
  </p>

  <h2>Two photographers, one street, two pictures</h2>
  <p>
    Two photographers stand at the same market stall at noon, both taking a portrait of the
    same vendor. The first opens the lens wide to f/1.8: the vendor's eyes are razor-sharp
    and the busy market behind her dissolves into soft colour. The second stops down to
    f/16: the vendor, the fruit, and the crowds three streets back are all crisp. Here is
    the puzzle: <em>both photographs come out exactly the same brightness.</em> Same light,
    same subject, same moment — different pictures. Neither is "more correct".
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="bet before you read on — why are both pictures equally bright?" widget="predict" config={expDuoPredict} />
  <p>
    The explanation is the technical core of photography: a "correct" exposure — the same
    amount of light recorded — can be made a thousand different ways, because a camera gives
    you three dials that each control the light <em>and</em> something else. Aperture also
    controls depth of field. Shutter speed also controls motion. ISO also controls noise.
    Which trade you make is not bookkeeping; it is the photograph's central creative
    decision. This page teaches the bookkeeping first — then the side effects that make it
    art.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="file each job under the dial that does it" widget="sort-buckets" config={expDialSort} />

  <h2>The idea: three dials, one bucket</h2>
  <p>
    The classic teaching analogy is a bucket under a tap. The <em>aperture</em> is the tap's
    width — a wide tap fills the bucket quickly; a narrow one trickles. The <em>shutter
    speed</em> is how long the tap runs. The bucket either fills exactly to the line (correct
    exposure), overflows (overexposed — highlights blown to pure white) or falls short
    (underexposed — shadows crushed to black). The <em>ISO</em> is stranger but essential: it
    is how sensitive your "taste test" is — the same half-full bucket is judged "correct" if
    you care less about completeness. Fill the bucket by many combinations of tap width and
    time and sensitivity: f/4 for 1/125 s at ISO 200, f/2.8 for 1/250 s at ISO 200, f/4 for
    1/250 s at ISO 400 — all the same brightness, all different photographs.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="three dials, one bucket, and stops" widget="equationStep" config={exposureCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the five cards walk the analogy, then break it in the
    right places. Card 2 contains the beginner's trap — f-numbers run <em>backwards</em>, and
    the reason is geometry, not perversity. Card 3 shows the shutter doing double duty: time
    controls both light and motion, which is why photographers speak of "freezing" a
    subject. Card 4 introduces the one dial that is not physics but bookkeeping — and its
    price. Card 5 is the punchline: because every dial can be measured in the same unit (the
    stop), the three dials form one exchange system — the "triangle" of the lesson's title.
  </p>

  <h2>The three dials: units, stops and the exchange rate</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Aperture</strong> is measured in f-numbers — the lens's focal length divided by
    the opening's diameter — and the scale runs backwards: <em>smaller</em> numbers mean
    <em>wider</em> openings. The reason is geometry: light-gathering scales with the
    opening's <em>area</em>, so f/2 (a 25 mm opening on a 50 mm lens) admits not twice but
    <em>four times</em> the light of f/4 (a 12.5 mm opening) — two "stops" of light. Each
    full stop on the standard scale (f/1.4, f/2, f/2.8, f/4, f/5.6, f/8, f/11, f/16, f/22)
    multiplies the opening's area by two, halving the light each step up. <strong>Shutter
    speed</strong> is simple time — 1 s, 1/2, 1/4, 1/8, 1/15, 1/30, 1/60, 1/125, 1/250,
    1/500, 1/1000 s — and each step to the right halves the light and halves the motion the
    frame can capture. <strong>ISO</strong> scales how brightly the sensor's signal is
    rendered — 100, 200, 400, 800, 1600, 3200 — each doubling of ISO is one stop of "free"
    brightness, bought at the price of amplified noise (grain in the shadows) and reduced
    highlight headroom.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="place the marker two stops wider than f/8" widget="place" config={expStopPlace} />
  <p>
    The universal currency across all three dials is the <strong>stop</strong>: one stop is a
    doubling or halving of light. The triangle's whole arithmetic is exchange in stops: open
    the aperture one stop and you may halve the shutter time, or drop the ISO one stop, and
    the bucket fills exactly as before. The slider bank below computes the exchange rate
    live.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="the exchange rate between the dials" widget="sliderLab" config={triangleCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> move one dial and the exposure value (EV) readout
    moves; move another dial the opposite way by the matching number of stops and the EV
    readout returns to where it was — that round trip <em>is</em> the triangle. Notice too
    that large apertures are the small f-numbers, and that the depth-of-field readout tracks
    the f-number directly: same currency, different side effect. Finally, watch how far ISO
    can be pushed when the other two dials are stuck — that is the escape hatch the next
    section uses at a concert.
  </p>
  <p>
    A worked example grounds the bookkeeping in a scene every photographer meets. The
    "Sunny 16" rule says: in bright daylight, f/16 with shutter speed equal to 1/ISO — f/16
    at 1/125 s with ISO 100 — gives correct exposure without a meter. But a portrait wants
    the background melted, so open to f/2.8: that is four stops wider (f/16 → f/11 → f/8 →
    f/5.6 → f/4 → f/2.8), so pay it back on the shutter — four stops faster: 1/125 → 1/2000
    s. Same bucket level, entirely different photograph: the portrait has razor-thin focus
    on the eyes and a dissolved background, while Sunny 16 had everything from nose to
    horizon sharp. Neither is "more correct"; the exchange just cashed the same light for
    different side effects.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="rebuild the Sunny 16 portrait exchange, step by step" widget="order" config={expSunnyOrder} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that a smaller f-number means a smaller opening — it
  looks plausible, because smaller numbers usually mean smaller things. But the f-number is
  a <em>ratio</em>: focal length divided by opening diameter. f/2 means the opening is one
  half the focal length across; f/16 means one sixteenth — much smaller. The ratio is why
  the same f-number means the same exposure on any lens, and why the numbers run backwards
  from the light.</p>

  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the bookkeeping is complete. You can read all three dials, count
    light in stops, and convert any correct exposure into any other — as the Sunny 16
    portrait showed. What made that exchange worth performing, though, was not the
    arithmetic: it was what the photographer bought with it. The side effects are the
    subject of the next section.
  </p>

  <h2>Depth of field and motion: the side effects you choose on purpose</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Depth of field</strong> (DOF) is the range of distances that appear acceptably
    sharp, and it obeys four levers. Aperture: wider openings (smaller f-numbers) produce
    shallower focus — the light cones from out-of-focus points are wider and blur faster.
    Focal length: longer lenses shallow the focus at the same aperture and framing. Subject
    distance: the closer you focus, the thinner the plane of sharpness falls. Sensor size
    acts through the others: larger sensors invite longer focal lengths for the same
    framing, and thus shallower DOF — the physical reason full-frame portraits melt
    backgrounds more readily than phone cameras do. The genres fork here on purpose:
    portraits at f/1.8 isolate a face from its world; landscapes at f/11 (often focused at
    the <strong>hyperfocal distance</strong>, which maximises the sharp range from
    foreground to infinity) keep everything crisp; street photographers pick f/8 and let the
    deep focus forgive imperfect timing. The out-of-focus quality itself — <em>bokeh</em> —
    has become a lens-design obsession, because "how it melts" is part of the image's
    character.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="pair each depth-of-field lever with its pull" widget="match" config={expDofMatch} />
  <p>
    <strong>Motion</strong> is the shutter's side effect, and it cuts both ways: blur as
    failure and blur as meaning. The freezing scale is roughly: 1/250 s handles walking
    people; 1/500–1/1000 s handles running and most sport; 1/2000 s and beyond freezes
    birds, water splashes and ball strikes. Handheld work adds the old reciprocal rule —
    keep the shutter no slower than 1/focal-length seconds (1/60 s on a 50 mm lens) unless
    stabilisation helps. But deliberate blur is a vocabulary: <strong>panning</strong>
    (following a cyclist at 1/30 s keeps the subject sharp while the world streaks — speed
    made visible), long exposures turn waterfalls to silk and traffic into light rivers
    (1/2 to 30 s, tripod mandatory, and in daylight a <strong>neutral-density filter</strong>
    — sunglasses for the lens — buys the missing stops), and star photography runs 10–25
    seconds before the Earth's rotation smears the points. The genres fork here too: the
    sports shooter pays everything for shutter speed (wide aperture, high ISO), the
    landscape shooter pays everything for depth and stillness (small aperture, ISO 100,
    tripod). Master photographers are not better at "correct" exposure — they are
    deliberate about which side effect to keep, and they know which dial to sacrifice first.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="drag the divider — blur as failure vs blur as meaning" widget="compare" config={expBlurCompare} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that blur is always a mistake and sharpness always a
  virtue. Blur is a vocabulary: the streaked background of a panned racing cyclist says
  "speed" in a way no frozen frame can; silky water says "endless flow". The mistake is not
  blur but <em>unintended</em> blur — which is why the skill runs in both directions:
  control the shutter deliberately, and know the freezing speeds well enough that what you
  keep is what you meant to keep.</p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that one of the three dials is "the right way" to
  expose, or that high ISO is a failure. In the triangle's logic there is no correct dial —
  only correct <em>side effects for this picture</em>. The concert photographer raising ISO
  to 6400 is not compensating for ignorance; grain that preserves a moment beats noise-free
  blur of nothing, which is why ISO is honoured as the escape dial rather than shamed.</p>

  <h2>Metering and light: the camera's opinion, overridden</h2>
  <p>
    Exposure needs a measurement, and the camera's built-in meter is a
    <em>reflected-light</em> meter: it reads the light bouncing off the scene and assumes the
    scene, on average, reflects middle grey (the standard is roughly 18 per cent
    reflectance). The assumption is brilliant for average scenes and systematically wrong at
    the extremes — which is why snow photographs come back grey (the meter underexposes the
    brilliant white toward grey: add about +2 stops of compensation) and why a night scene
    or a black cat washes out (subtract compensation). Camera metering modes operationalise
    different opinions: <strong>evaluative/matrix</strong> metering reads the whole frame
    against a database of scenes; <strong>centre-weighted</strong> privileges the middle;
    <strong>spot</strong> meters a tiny circle — the tool for deliberately placing one tone
    (meter the highlight, open up; meter the shadow, stop down). The photographer's working
    instrument is <strong>exposure compensation</strong> (+/− in stops), nudging every
    automatic exposure off the meter's grey assumption — and the feedback loop is the
    <strong>histogram</strong>, the brightness distribution graphed left (black) to right
    (white): a histogram bunched against the right edge means clipped highlights (data gone
    forever), against the left means crushed shadows. Advanced practice "exposes to the
    right" — pushing the histogram as far toward the highlight edge as data survives,
    because highlights are harder to recover than shadows — and bracketing (shooting at −1,
    0, +1) hedges high-contrast scenes, feeding the HDR merge.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="find the faulty line in this student's metering notes" widget="spot-error" config={expMeterSpot} />
  <p>
    The scene's <strong>dynamic range</strong> — the span between its brightest meaningful
    highlight and deepest meaningful shadow — is the quiet variable that makes metering
    hard: a backlit portrait or a sunset interior can span 12–14 stops while the sensor
    captures maybe 10–14, so something must be sacrificed or blended. Ansel Adams built the
    <strong>Zone System</strong> on exactly this problem — placing shadows where the film
    would hold them and developing (in film chemistry) to control the highlights — and
    digital exposure inherits its logic: decide what must hold detail, meter for it, let the
    rest fall where it falls. In practice the triangle sets the physics, the meter guesses
    the scene, the histogram reports the truth, and the artist decides who is right — the
    daily loop of every deliberate photograph.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that the camera's automatic exposure is an objective
  measurement of the scene, so a grey snow picture or a washed-out night shot means the
  camera is faulty. The meter is an opinion with a fixed assumption — "make what I measure
  average out to middle grey" — and it is the assumption, not the hardware, that fails on
  scenes dominated by very bright or very dark subjects. The fix is not repair but
  override: exposure compensation exists precisely because you, not the meter, know whether
  this scene <em>should</em> be grey.</p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="true or false — trusting the meter" widget="judge" config={expGreyJudge} />

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Your meter says f/8 at 1/125 s, ISO 100. You want a shallower background and switch to f/2.8. What shutter speed keeps the exposure identical?</li>
    <li>Why does the same f-number deliver the same exposure on a 50 mm lens and a 200 mm lens, even though the 200 mm opening is physically larger?</li>
    <li>Your photograph of a skier against snow comes out underexposed and grey. What happened, and what is the fix?</li>
    <li>A histogram bunches against the right edge. What does that tell you, and why can't editing fully undo it?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) f/8 → f/5.6 → f/4 → f/2.8 is three stops wider, so pay
    back three stops of shutter: 1/125 → 1/250 → 1/500 → 1/1000 s. (2) Because the
    f-number is a ratio — focal length divided by diameter. The 200 mm lens's opening is
    proportionally the same fraction of its focal length, and exposure depends on brightness
    per area of the image, which the ratio fixes. (3) The meter assumed the bright snow
    should be middle grey and underexposed everything toward that average. Add roughly +2
    stops of exposure compensation — you are overriding the meter's grey assumption, not
    correcting a fault. (4) Highlights are clipped: sensor sites recorded pure white with no
    detail above. Nothing in the file can be recovered that was never recorded — which is
    why exposing to protect highlights (and bracketing) is the professional reflex.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Exposure is one budget spent through three dials: aperture (light + depth of field), shutter speed (light + motion), ISO (brightness + noise).</li>
    <li>f-numbers are ratios and run backwards: smaller number, wider opening, and each full stop halves or doubles the light.</li>
    <li>The stop is the universal currency: any correct exposure can be traded across dials stop for stop (Sunny 16 → portrait at f/2.8, 1/2000 s).</li>
    <li>Side effects are the creative decision: f/1.8 melts backgrounds, f/11 holds landscapes, 1/2000 s freezes birds, 1/30 s panning writes "speed".</li>
    <li>The meter assumes middle grey and is systematically wrong at the extremes; compensation, spot metering and the histogram put the decision back with you.</li>
    <li>Highlights clip permanently; shadows survive better — so protect the right edge of the histogram.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The triangle is the grammar; everything after is composition and light. Within the wider
    atlas, the physics lessons on light and optics explain what the lens is doing to the rays
    (and why the f-number geometry works), and the art-history lessons train the eye that
    decides <em>which</em> side effect a picture needs. Later chapters of photographic
    practice — flash, colour and the digital darkroom — all inherit this page's one constant:
    stops are the currency, and every dial's side effect is a choice.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    One more worked metering passage shows the grey-assumption logic under pressure, because
    spot metering is where the photographer finally outruns the camera. A stage scene: a
    singer lit by a single spotlight against a black theatre. Matrix metering averages the
    vast black with the lit face and overexposes badly — the face blows, the black turns
    grey murk. The disciplined alternative: switch to spot metering, take a reading from the
    singer's lit cheek (a Caucasian skin tone sits roughly one stop brighter than middle
    grey, so the meter's "make it grey" instruction means the photographer opens about one
    stop to restore the skin to skin), lock that exposure, and let the background fall to
    true black — which is not underexposure but the photograph's intent. Dark skin reverses
    the correction; snow and bridal dresses repeat the first lesson at +2; a grey card held
    in the subject's light eliminates the guesswork entirely, because the card <em>is</em>
    the middle grey the meter seeks. Every one of these moves is the same sentence: the
    meter reports how to make the measured surface grey, and the photographer decides which
    surfaces deserve to stay what they are.
  </p>
  <p>
    Four refinements carry the triangle into advanced work. <strong>The EV system</strong>:
    exposure value condenses the dials into one number — EV = log<sub>2</sub>(N²/t), with N
    the f-number and t the shutter time in seconds — and the slider bank's EV readout is
    this arithmetic at work; light meters, flash guides and old cameras all speak it, and
    "EV 15" is the formal name of Sunny 16. <strong>Reciprocity and its failure</strong>:
    the equivalence principle (a stop here buys a stop there) holds tightly for digital
    sensors across their working range, but film breaks down at very long exposures —
    <em>reciprocity failure</em> demands extra exposure past a second or so — and digital
    has its own edge effects: long-exposure heat noise, and electronic shutters introducing
    rolling-shutter distortion at fast pan speeds. <strong>Sensor formats and
    equivalence</strong>: the same f-number admits the same light <em>per area</em>, but a
    larger sensor's total area gathers more total light at the same exposure — which is why
    full-frame images are cleaner at the same ISO, why "equivalent aperture" arguments about
    DOF and noise rage online, and why the triangle's dials must be quoted with the sensor
    they belong to for strict comparisons. <strong>Flash changes the game</strong>: a flash
    pulse freezes motion itself (duration around 1/1000 s), the shutter no longer controls
    ambient-and-flash separately (the sync-speed limit appears), and the "triangle" becomes
    a tetrahedron of ambient exposure plus flash power — the reason studio photographers
    think in f-stops and power ratios, not shutter speeds. The constants through all of it
    are the ones this page taught: stops are the currency, every dial's side effect is a
    creative choice, the meter is an opinion about grey, and the histogram is the only
    witness that does not flatter anyone.
  </p>

